---
code: verdict-terminal-pipeline
when-fires: a `__is_converged` body's last status-bearing statement is a PIPELINE, so the answering status is the tail stage's. lint/source_verdict.rs. No params - the span says which body.
why: rul-rc-partition - a verdict function's exit status reads one fixed table, so a tail stage's status flattens `1 = complement` and `>=2 = cannot-say` into whatever the tail produced. FALSIFICATION-FIRST (rul-unprovable-rides-the-vouch): the check may under-report, never gate.
---
-- oracle.sh --
# dorc-lang/v0.2
foo__is_converged() {
foo --status "$1" | grep -q ok
}

-- replay --
$ dorc lint oracle.sh --no-tools
dorc lint findings (advisory; the machine format `--format=jsonl` is the stable surface):

oracle.sh:
  3:1 warn [verdict-body:verdict-terminal-pipeline] this __is_converged body answers with a pipeline, so the status Dorc reads is the final stage's, not the described tool's own -- the difference between `does not hold` (status 1) and `cannot say` (2 and up) flattens into whatever the tail exits; prefer a shape where the tool's own status is the body's answer.

dorc lint: 0 errors, 1 warning, 0 infos across 1 file.

$ dorc-loom --this vars
case: verdict-terminal-pipeline
{{v0}} = "0"
{{v1}} = "s"
{{v2}} = "1"
{{v3}} = ""
{{v4}} = "0"
{{v5}} = "s"
{{v6}} = "1"
{{v7}} = ""
